```

**Claim**: `flex`, `xflex`, `yflex`, `filler` are available for responsive layouts; `xflex` is especially useful for compare pane bodies.

**Evidence** ([elements.hpp](https://github.com/ArthurSonzogni/FTXUI/blob/a9ddb312f9b00dd492ba73b5394a72c601ef2c94/include/ftxui/dom/elements.hpp#L156-L177)):

```cpp
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element flex(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element flex_grow(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element flex_shrink(Element);

FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM)
Element xflex(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM)
Element xflex_grow(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM)
Element xflex_shrink(Element);

FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM)
Element yflex(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM)
Element yflex_grow(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM)
Element yflex_shrink(Element);

FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element notflex(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element filler();
```

**Claim**: `frame`, `xframe`, `yframe`, plus scroll indicators, are available for content larger than the visible pane.

**Evidence** ([elements.hpp](https://github.com/ArthurSonzogni/FTXUI/blob/a9ddb312f9b00dd492ba73b5394a72c601ef2c94/include/ftxui/dom/elements.hpp#L184-L201)):

```cpp
// --- Frame ---
// A frame is a scrollable area. The internal area is potentially larger than
// the external one. The internal area is scrolled in order to make visible the
// focused element.
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element frame(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element xframe(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element yframe(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element focus(Element);

// --- Misc ---
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element vscroll_indicator(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element hscroll_indicator(Element);
```

**Claim**: Alignment helpers include `hcenter`, `vcenter`, `center`, and `align_right`, useful for headers and numeric columns.

**Evidence** ([elements.hpp](https://github.com/ArthurSonzogni/FTXUI/blob/a9ddb312f9b00dd492ba73b5394a72c601ef2c94/include/ftxui/dom/elements.hpp#L208-L212)):

```cpp
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element hcenter(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element vcenter(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element center(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element align_right(Element);
FTXUI_EXPORT(DOM) Element nothing(Element element);
```

**Terminal width caveat**: `Dimension::Full()` uses full terminal width/height; `Dimension::Fit(element)` sizes the screen to the element. Wide tables may exceed terminal width if rendered with `Dimension::Fit(document, true)`.

**Evidence** ([doc/module-screen.md](https://github.com/ArthurSonzogni/FTXUI/blob/a9ddb312f9b00dd492ba73b5394a72c601ef2c94/doc/module-screen.md#L78-L96)):

```md
The @ref ftxui::Dimension utility controls screen sizing:

* `Dimension::Full()` — use full terminal width or height
* `Dimension::Fit(element)` — size to fit the rendered @ref ftxui::Element
* `Dimension::Fixed(n)` — use exactly `n` columns or rows

These values are to be passed to `ftxui::Screen::Create()`.

@ref ftxui::Screen::Create() provides two overloads:

- `Screen::Create(Dimension)` sets both width and height to the same kind of dimension
- `Screen::Create(Dimension width, Dimension height)` allows distinct control per axis
```

**Pattern**: For live terminal apps, use full width and fit height, as in the package-manager and graph examples.

**Evidence** ([examples/dom/package_manager.cpp](https://github.com/ArthurSonzogni/FTXUI/blob/a9ddb312f9b00dd492ba73b5394a72c601ef2c94/examples/dom/package_manager.cpp#L129-L133)):

```cpp
auto document = render();
auto screen = Screen::Create(Dimension::Full(), Dimension::Fit(document));
Render(screen, document);
std::cout << reset_position;
screen.Print();
```

---

## Feasible monochrome compare-pane layout patterns

### Option A — Table-first comparison

Best for: many attributes, side-by-side values, stable column widths.

```cpp
auto table = Table({
  {"Metric", "A", "B", "Delta"},
  {"Latency", "120ms", "95ms", "-25ms"},
  {"Memory", "42MB", "51MB", "+9MB"},
});

table.SelectAll().Border(LIGHT);
table.SelectRow(0).Border(DOUBLE);
table.SelectRow(0).Decorate(bold);
table.SelectColumn(1).DecorateCells(align_right);
table.SelectColumn(2).DecorateCells(align_right);
table.SelectColumn(3).DecorateCells(align_right);

auto document = table.Render() | frame | border;
```

Grounded by `Table` selection/border/alignment APIs above.

### Option B — Split-pane summary + bars

Best for: top-level comparison dashboard.

```cpp
hbox({
  window(text(" A "), left_summary) | xflex,
  separator(),
  window(text(" B "), right_summary) | xflex,
  separator(),
  window(text(" Delta "), vbox(delta_rows)) | xflex,
}) | border;
```

For each row:

```cpp
hbox({
  text("CPU") | size(WIDTH, EQUAL, 12),
  separator(),
  gauge(cpu_ratio) | flex,
  separator(),
  text("72%") | align_right | size(WIDTH, EQUAL, 6),
});
```

Grounded by `hbox`/`vbox`/`separator`/`gauge`/`size`/`flex` APIs above.

### Option C — Trend panes with graph

Best for: sparkline-ish time series, throughput, latency over time.

```cpp
hbox({
  window(text(" Before "), graph(before_fn) | flex) | flex,
  separator(),
  window(text(" After "), graph(after_fn) | flex) | flex,
}) | border;
```

Caveat: ensure each graph function returns `width` samples for the requested width, because FTXUI calls `graph_function_(width, height)` with doubled logical resolution.

### Option D — Dense list with fixed labels and flexible body

Best for: terminal widths that vary; good fallback when tables get too wide.

```cpp
vbox({
  hbox({ text("Latency") | size(WIDTH, EQUAL, 12), separator(), gauge(lat) | flex }),
  separator(),
  hbox({ text("Memory")  | size(WIDTH, EQUAL, 12), separator(), gauge(mem) | flex }),
}) | xflex | size(WIDTH, GREATER_THAN, 40) | border;
```

Grounded by the gallery `Wrap()` pattern and package-manager row pattern.

## Main caveats for your downstream design recommendation

1. **Avoid color-dependent semantics**: Official examples often use `color(...)`, but the same layouts work with borders, separators, `bold`, `dim`, fixed widths, and alignment.
2. **Tables can become wider than terminal**: Use `frame`/`xframe`, abbreviated labels, or a list layout for narrow terminals.
3. **`graph` needs height**: It has minimum `3x3`, but practical readability needs more vertical space.
4. **`gauge` is ideal for normalized scalar bars**: It clamps to `[0,1]`, uses block characters, and works without color.
5. **Use `Dimension::Full()` for terminal-width apps**: Prefer `Screen::Create(Dimension::Full(), Dimension::Fit(document))` or component `App::Fullscreen()`/`App::FitComponent()` depending on whether the app is DOM-only or interactive.
6. **For compare panes, prefer structural encoding**: border style, separator style, ordering, fixed label widths, right-aligned numbers, and gauges are all viable without per-element colors.

