### 2. `size(WIDTH, EQUAL, width)` sets the required width and disables flex on that axis

**Claim:** `size(WIDTH, EQUAL, width)` forces the element’s width requirement to `width` and sets both `flex_grow_x` and `flex_shrink_x` to `0`.

**Evidence** ([source](https://github.com/ArthurSonzogni/FTXUI/blob/a9ddb312f9b00dd492ba73b5394a72c601ef2c94/src/ftxui/dom/size.cpp#L24-L49)):

```cpp
void ComputeRequirement() override {
  Node::ComputeRequirement();
  requirement_ = children_[0]->requirement();

  auto& value = direction_ == WIDTH ? requirement_.min_x : requirement_.min_y;

  switch (constraint_) {
    case LESS_THAN:
      value = std::min(value, value_);
      break;
    case EQUAL:
      value = value_;
      break;
    case GREATER_THAN:
      value = std::max(value, value_);
      break;
  }

  if (direction_ == WIDTH) {
    requirement_.flex_grow_x = 0;
    requirement_.flex_shrink_x = 0;
  } else {
    requirement_.flex_grow_y = 0;
    requirement_.flex_shrink_y = 0;
  }
}
```

**Implication:** If the detail pane is decorated as:

```cpp
detail | flex | size(WIDTH, EQUAL, width)
```

the outer `size` wins for width and removes horizontal flex. The pane becomes fixed-width, so it will not expand to consume remaining columns.

